edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1045 "The Vector Equation of a Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120016 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8313edbb-dabc-5be6-9269-6a1622b71d3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,-3,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee828cf7-8b80-575c-a644-51d699464b23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120224 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,-3,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(3,2,-8)$ and $B(1,2,-5)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,2,-5⟩-⟨3,2,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,3⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120711 #edb/ref 1061 17917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf165cc4-ae6c-564d-b8e6-051c7aba1faa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120712 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120712 #edb/ref 1061 17918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e689c57c-a11c-582d-bed2-1b5777ad003d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120713 #edb/ref 1061 17919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36217cb6-2731-5a89-84ce-0738c511b417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120714 #edb/ref 1061 17921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b13bc4d-57be-5d17-841a-4f2645bc3982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120870 #edb/ref 1061 38733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69d22458-d2d5-5c3d-b6fb-4714f2ea1b49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121049 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121049 #edb/ref 1065 3255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121049 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121049 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121120 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121120 #edb/ref 1065 6411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121120 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121120 #edb/ref 1067 "A Vector Parallel To a Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1066db60-12af-5e82-a7c0-d24afa450e35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "336d23d9-d848-54ca-86e6-782c1b13a369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,4⟩=22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65e4dacb-b64f-5663-ae54-844ec9e37c4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6bfd83ea-5bb5-57e7-b814-e0e365ce1b44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261298 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩=12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261298 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261298 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a344d6d0-c843-57a0-87ed-6643487a6ce9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261313 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,1⟩=18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5b752a4-52ea-54e2-96dc-81dc14098d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261371 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,-2⟩=8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa9815dd-2b59-5495-91a4-c387e6882786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261577 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261577 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261577 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0f71bee-c234-59c3-9e78-d739bf030e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261744 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261744 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261744 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d4093fa7-7b24-5445-bf63-718de7a82e0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261981 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f03309e1-ca15-5335-aab5-a2535a4337a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186261577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262265 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e498358-75ca-5ef1-ae02-06dee6634086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262547 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f46674a6-7e2b-5656-b018-7f5bf5e7afa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1071 "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa3bed00-1af4-5c46-8c12-264209634c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & ={t}_{2}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨3,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7885abf5-baa7-59dd-be20-bec3451c5be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393043 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186393049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393044 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "31ef96c8-9198-5e30-8832-aa55a71dc6d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393045 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=⟨-3,4,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "493da2a3-c2b1-5d90-946c-32226dc19e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393046 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=⟨3,-5,-2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218